] Swimwear + quick-dry towel\n- [ ] Comfortable walking shoes (expect 12–18k steps/day) + sandals or thongs\n- [ ] Sun hat and sunglasses\n- [ ] Sleepwear, underwear, socks for 3–4 days\n\n## Packing — essentials\n- [ ] SPF 50+ sunscreen (Australian UV is genuinely harsh — reapply)\n- [ ] Reusable water bottle (tap water is excellent; refill stations are common)\n- [ ] Type I power adapter (Australia) + multi-port USB charger\n- [ ] Power bank\n- [ ] Basic meds: painkillers, antihistamine, blister plasters, motion-sickness tablets if doing a boat trip\n- [ ] Daypack for coastal walks\n- [ ] Insect repellent (useful at dusk near parks/water)\n\n## Documents\n- [ ] Passports + ETA confirmation\n- [ ] Printed and digital copies of bookings\n- [ ] Insurance policy number and emergency line\n- [ ] Driver's licence if hiring a car (left-hand-side driving)\n- [ ] Two payment cards stored separately; a little cash for markets\n\n## On arrival\n- [ ] Buy/activate eSIM or confirm roaming\n- [ ] Tap on at the station with phone/card — no need to buy a separate Opal card\n- [ ] Note: Australia drives on the left; look right when crossing\n- [ ] Emergency number is 000\n- [ ] Tipping is not expected\n\n## Good to know\n- Ferries are public transport, not just tourist boats — the Manly and Watsons Bay\n  runs are the cheapest harbour cruise you'll find.\n- Opal daily fare caps mean heavy transit days are cheaper than they look;\n  Sundays have an especially low all-day cap.\n- Surf beaches: swim between the red-and-yellow flags only.\n"
